From a2de76768e1b768f98ae48dd24d6fefcbd2762ed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mudb0y <stasprzecinek@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 11:00:32 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] Say in eci.h which thread runs the callback

The header said the callback runs on the engine's own synthesis thread.
It runs on the caller's, inside eciSpeaking or eciSynchronize, which is
what docs/api.md says and what the NVDA driver relies on: its callback
feeds the player from the thread that called eciSynchronize, and in
NVDA's process on Linux all 23 callbacks of a two-item utterance that
handed on audio or a mark came on that thread. Someone
writing a caller from the header alone would have guarded against the
wrong thread and could have waited for callbacks that never come.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 include/eci.h | 8 +++++---
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/include/eci.h b/include/eci.h
index e1fcdf9..24d2b61 100644
--- a/include/eci.h
+++ b/include/eci.h
@@ -112,9 +112,11 @@ typedef enum {
  * it: reading eight bytes where four were written reads the register's other
  * half.
  *
- * It runs on the engine's own synthesis thread, not the caller's. What it may
- * do is copy the samples and return; what it may not do is call back into the
- * same instance. */
+ * It runs on the caller's thread that is inside eciSpeaking or
+ * eciSynchronize, not on the engine's own synthesis thread, which posts each
+ * buffer and waits for the answer: a caller that synthesises and then neither
+ * asks nor waits is never called back. What it may do is copy the samples and
+ * return; what it may not do is call back into the same instance. */
 typedef int (ECICALL *ECICallback)(ECIHand handle, ECIMessage message,
                                    int param, void *data);
 
